{"name":"Pallid Blue","hex":"#b3cdd4","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pallid","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Appearing weak, pale, or wan."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pallid"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Euphilotes pallescens","blocks":[{"text":"Euphilotes pallescens, the pale blue, pallid blue or pallid dotted blue, is a species of butterfly of the family Lycaenidae. It is found in the United States in southeastern California, Nevada, southern Utah and northern Arizona."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b8/Sand_Mountain_Blue_Butterfly.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euphilotes_pallescens","wikidata":"Q18324975"},{"title":"Pallor","blocks":[{"text":"Pallor is a pale color of the skin that can be caused by illness, emotional shock or stress, stimulant use, or anemia, and is the result of a reduced amount of oxyhaemoglobin and may also be visible as pallor of the conjunctivae of the eyes on physical examination."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Scarlet_fever_1.JPG/330px-Scarlet_fever_1.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pallor","wikidata":"Q2336398"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/PallorSkinTones.jpg/500px-PallorSkinTones.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/PallorSkinTones.jpg/500px-PallorSkinTones.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Effects of different skin tone on the appearance of pallor","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APallorSkinTones.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Richard_Parkes_Bonington_by_Margaret_Sarah_Carpenter_%28n%C3%A9e_Geddes%29.jpg/500px-Richard_Parkes_Bonington_by_Margaret_Sarah_Carpenter_%28n%C3%A9e_Geddes%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Richard_Parkes_Bonington_by_Margaret_Sarah_Carpenter_%28n%C3%A9e_Geddes%29.jpg/1280px-Richard_Parkes_Bonington_by_Margaret_Sarah_Carpenter_%28n%C3%A9e_Geddes%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Richard_Parkes_Bonington_by_Margaret_Sarah_Carpenter_%28n%C3%A9e_Geddes%29.jpg/500px-Richard_Park